{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Multi-Token Prediction (MTP) Notebook\n\n> Hands-on Build It and Exercises."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Build It"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`code/main.py` builds a single MTP module end to end: shared embedding, projection, transformer block, shared output head. It then computes the per-depth cross-entropy loss on a short synthetic sequence and prints the parameter count by component. A toy vocabulary of 32 tokens keeps the numbers readable."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 1: shared embedding table"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "A single `vocab_size x hidden` table is used by the main model AND by every MTP module at every depth. Not a second copy \u2014 literally the same tensor."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 2: the per-depth combination"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def combine(prev_hidden, next_token_embed, M_k):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    # concat along feature dim, then project down to hidden"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    concat = rms_norm(prev_hidden) + rms_norm(next_token_embed)  # vector addition stand-in"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    projected = matvec(M_k, concat)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return projected"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Real DeepSeek-V3 concatenates the two RMSNormed vectors to `[2h]` and projects with an `h x 2h` matrix. The toy uses vector addition for stdlib brevity."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 3: the transformer block at depth k"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Self-attention plus MLP. In the toy, a one-layer linear attention block and a SwiGLU MLP keep the structure visible without numpy."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 4: the shared output head"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Reuse the main model's output projection. Logits over the vocabulary."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 5: per-depth loss"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Cross-entropy of softmax(logits) against the ground-truth token at offset `k`. Aggregate across depths with the `lambda / D` scaling factor."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 6: parameter accounting"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Print the total parameter count, the shared (embedding, head) count, and the per-module extra count. Show the ratio of MTP extra to main-model size."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. Run `code/main.py`. Show the per-depth loss decreases monotonically as the synthetic signal strengthens. Modify the synthetic to use a fixed pattern and verify both depth-1 and depth-2 losses converge.\n\n2. Compute the parameter overhead for a dense 70B model (hidden 8192, 80 layers) with D=1 MTP module. Compare to the DeepSeek-V3 reported 14B overhead. Explain why DeepSeek's number is higher: the MTP transformer block inherits the same MoE structure, inflating the per-module parameter count.\n\n3. Implement D=2 in the toy: add a second MTP module that takes h^(1) and predicts `t_{i+2}`. Verify the joint loss and the parameter accounting match the DeepSeek paper's equations 19-21.\n\n4. Switch the toy to parallel MTP (Gloeckle-style): add D output heads on top of the main hidden state, each predicting a different offset. Measure how the losses per depth compare to the sequential version on the same synthetic signal. The sequential version should produce lower depth-k loss for k > 1 because it conditions on the intermediate predictions.\n\n5. Use the trained MTP module as an EAGLE-style draft: call module k to propose `t_{i+k}` at inference. Measure the acceptance rate of these draft tokens against the main model's predictions on a held-out sequence. If you hit 50%+ on the toy, you have reproduced the empirical MTP-as-draft property."
      ]
    }
  ],
